WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.300
If wolves eat moose, why don't the wolves eat every last moose?

2
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:11.600
On one real island in Lake Superior, they never have.

3
00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:15.440
Let's find out why, starting with a model island.

4
00:00:16.533 --> 00:00:22.973
First, two words. A population is all the members of one species living in one

5
00:00:22.973 --> 00:00:27.433
place. All the moose on our island make one population.

6
00:00:28.633 --> 00:00:29.893
All the wolves make another.

7
00:00:31.017 --> 00:00:34.977
A predator is an animal that catches and eats other animals.

8
00:00:36.177 --> 00:00:37.777
The animal it eats is its prey.

9
00:00:39.057 --> 00:00:42.297
Here, the wolves are the predators, and the moose are their prey.

10
00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:45.750
Moose eat young trees and other plants.

11
00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:51.250
Wolves eat moose. We draw arrows to show who eats whom.

12
00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:54.390
Each arrow points from the food to the eater.

13
00:00:55.433 --> 00:00:57.013
Now we need one number.

14
00:00:58.413 --> 00:00:59.573
Count the moose for each wolf.

15
00:01:00.793 --> 00:01:02.533
The counter in the corner keeps track.

16
00:01:03.773 --> 00:01:06.653
It is a rough clue to how easily wolves find food.

17
00:01:07.717 --> 00:01:11.437
Our model island starts with many moose and only a few wolves.

18
00:01:12.657 --> 00:01:13.757
The counter is high.

19
00:01:15.057 --> 00:01:18.757
In this model, that means hunting is easy, and the wolves eat well.

20
00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:23.480
With plenty of food, more wolf pups survive and grow up.

21
00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:26.240
Year by year, there are more wolves.

22
00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:29.060
And more wolves catch more moose.

23
00:01:30.033 --> 00:01:31.853
So the moose population falls.

24
00:01:33.273 --> 00:01:34.333
Watch the counter drop.

25
00:01:35.653 --> 00:01:37.813
Now each wolf has only a few moose to hunt.

26
00:01:38.867 --> 00:01:40.687
Here is a question for you.

27
00:01:41.767 --> 00:01:43.507
There are now only ten moose for each wolf.

28
00:01:44.887 --> 00:01:46.387
What happens to the wolves next?

29
00:01:47.767 --> 00:01:48.767
Pause and predict.

30
00:01:55.133 --> 00:01:57.533
In this model, that number is too low.

31
00:01:59.093 --> 00:02:01.713
Wolves catch too little food, and fewer pups survive.

32
00:02:03.053 --> 00:02:04.433
The wolf population falls.

33
00:02:05.733 --> 00:02:06.733
But look at the moose.

34
00:02:07.913 --> 00:02:09.173
Many moose are still alive.

35
00:02:10.233 --> 00:02:13.313
With fewer wolves hunting, more moose calves survive.

36
00:02:14.693 --> 00:02:16.473
The moose population grows again.

37
00:02:17.593 --> 00:02:20.533
The counter climbs back up, and the loop can start over.

38
00:02:21.567 --> 00:02:25.367
This rule fits a predator that eats mostly one kind of prey.

39
00:02:26.687 --> 00:02:29.507
On an island, neither animal can easily leave.

40
00:02:30.747 --> 00:02:35.387
A predator with other food may not go hungry, and it could keep eating a prey down

41
00:02:35.387 --> 00:02:36.387
to nothing.

42
00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:39.030
A real island is messier.

43
00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:42.570
Weather, ticks and disease also change the counts.

44
00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:47.150
There, this number alone cannot show that wolves are hungry.

45
00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:50.550
You will see real counts in the second film.

46
00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:53.910
So here is the answer to our question.

47
00:02:55.270 --> 00:02:59.150
When there are few moose for each wolf, wolves tend to catch moose less often.

48
00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:03.130
If they get too little food, fewer wolves survive.

49
00:03:04.630 --> 00:03:07.830
Wolves usually run short of food before the moose run out.
